**4.3.2 Design philosophy (HMI style guide)**
The HMI design philosophy document shall define the HMI design requirements. The HMI design philosophy shall include, at a minimum, the following:

a) Scope and objectives of the HMI;
b) Standard definitions and terminology;
c) Roles and responsibilities for HMI development, implementation, and maintenance;
d) Design process;
e) HMI management of change (MOC) process;
f) HMI training requirements for operators, maintainers, and developers;
g) HMI performance monitoring and continuous improvement;
h) HMI lifecycle management; and
i) References to HMI style guide(s).

**4.3.3 HMI style guide**
The HMI style guide(s) shall be developed based on the HMI design philosophy. The style guide(s) shall provide the specific technical requirements for the HMI implementation. The HMI style guide shall include, at a minimum, the following:

a) Display hierarchy (e.g., levels of displays);
b) Display navigation methods;
c) Color palette and meaning/usage (e.g., alarm states, process states, equipment status);
d) Symbols and object libraries (e.g., pumps, valves, sensors);
e) Graphic and animation behavior (e.g., movement, rotation, visibility);
f) Alarm indication and management in the HMI;
g) Data entry, control actions, and security/access;
h) Trending and historical data presentation;
i) Fonts, text, and labels; and
j) Unit of measure conventions.

**4.4 HMI development and implementation process**
The HMI development and implementation process should be integrated with the overall process control system design, development, and implementation lifecycle. Figure 3 shows the HMI lifecycle phases.

**4.4.1 HMI requirements**
HMI requirements shall be established by defining the functional and operational needs of the user (e.g., operator, maintenance). These requirements shall be derived from:

a) Process hazards analysis (PHA) and risk assessments;
b) Operational goals and objectives;
c) Operating procedures (standard and abnormal);
d) Control system functional requirements; and
e) Human factors engineering principles.

**4.4.2 Design and implementation**
HMI design and implementation shall follow the HMI design philosophy and the HMI style guide(s). The design shall be verified against the HMI requirements.

**4.4.3 HMI validation**
HMI validation shall be performed to ensure that the HMI meets the HMI requirements and supports the operator in the safe and effective management of the process. Validation shall be performed prior to putting the HMI into service (e.g., during the FAT/SAT or commissioning). Validation methods may include:

a) Walkthroughs/reviews with experienced operators;
b) Simulation-based testing;
c) Training sessions;
d) Usability testing; and
e) Testing with abnormal conditions (e.g., alarm floods).
